#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7223f4c-b6aa-5e20-8f6b-1f333bac0da6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "511feb42-d0dd-55b6-85c5-f0605fcf8da4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9454" :question/problem "A body of mass $3 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =t^2- e^{t-4}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=4$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - e^{t - 4}) \\\\\n&= 2t - e^{t - 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at\n\n$$\nt=4 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= 2(4) - e^{4 - 4} \\\\\n&= 8 - 1 \\\\\n&= 7m/s^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot (7) \\\\\n&= 21\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}F & = & 21\\end{vmatrix} = 21 \\, \\textrm{N}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151976 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/id #uuid "3ecb82f0-3877-5732-851d-adec73abb890" :question/math-academy-id "e-9859" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t$." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t)$, we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{3t - 6} + t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 3e^{3t - 6} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3e^{3t - 6} + 2t) \\\\\n&= 9e^{3t - 6} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (9e^{3t - 6} + 2) \\\\\n&= 18e^{3t - 6} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151977 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/id #uuid "2666ce7a-356a-574a-88fd-97417afc8ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-9860" :question/problem "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 12t^{2} - 30t + 5) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 24t - 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6t^{2} - 24t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2} - 4t - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 1)(t - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe disregard the negative solution.\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when\n\n$$\nt=5 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b58342de-c454-5afe-9ecb-ccafd98db48e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149059} {:answer/id #uuid "d43ff081-53ff-551e-9f2a-b0dc4ac8ae7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149060} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} {:answer/id #uuid "1eda97be-7632-50f1-89a9-15ea96362e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 1s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149062} {:answer/id #uuid "7484898e-4401-56e2-b0e3-591df12b1c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 6s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b2fc05-bb67-59ca-b877-9588be0e5e39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149061} :answer-field/id #uuid "977bf223-9796-5239-a7a2-1373f812b958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149058}] :question/id #uuid "396d3531-2c0c-5e02-bb04-58dd2fc7f372" :question/math-academy-id "q-138769" :question/problem "A body of mass $2kg$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\n\nAt time $t \\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) = 2t^{3} - 3t^{2} - 36t + 2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5048da6-f06e-54ef-99f3-02d35784d53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 7s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149065} {:answer/id #uuid "f141134b-33f4-5535-a223-d62b5f9b2448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 3s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149066} {:answer/id #uuid "06ce788d-868d-5cf0-b75c-d08a7ee4d31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 4s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149067} {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} {:answer/id #uuid "60a06afb-6930-5bb6-9a10-096b29ce56c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 5s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55869669-22e0-5ae3-a4b8-017647f5cb30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t = 2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149068} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca7066d7-ace6-51fb-8248-23eb546ef0b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149064}] :question/id #uuid "8295ff34-59d6-595e-a3a0-97b1c540771c" :question/math-academy-id "q-138770" :question/problem "A body of mass $1.5kg$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\n\nAt time $t \\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = 2t^{3} - 12t^{2} + t - 5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude."}]}
